A '''universal health care''' or '''universal health coverage''' ('''UHC''') system is a [[healthcare system]] in which all citizens of a country have some level of [[health insurance|health coverage]].<ref name="w728">{{cite journal | last1=Endalamaw | first1=Aklilu | last2=Mengistu | first2=Tesfaye Setegn | last3=Khatri | first3=Resham B. | last4=Wolka | first4=Eskinder | last5=Erku | first5=Daniel | last6=Zewdie | first6=Anteneh | last7=Assefa | first7=Yibeltal | title=Universal health coverage—Exploring the what, how, and why using realist review | journal=PLOS Global Public Health | volume=5 | issue=3 | date=2025-03-18 | issn=2767-3375 | pmid=40100795 | pmc=11918392 | doi=10.1371/journal.pgph.0003330 | doi-access=free | article-number=e0003330}}</ref>  

While often confused with [[single-payer healthcare]] (in which the government provides health coverage for all citizens),<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Liu |first=Jodi L. |last2=Brook |first2=Robert H. |date=July 2017|title=What is Single-Payer Health Care? A Review of Definitions and Proposals in the U.S |url=https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28493177 |journal=Journal of General Internal Medicine |volume=32 |issue=7 |pages=822–831 |doi=10.1007/s11606-017-4063-5 |issn=1525-1497 |pmc=5481251 |pmid=28493177}}</ref> universal health coverage can be achieved through many different models, and only 13 of the [[OECD]]'s 34 countries used single-payer models as of 2013.<ref name=":5">{{Cite journal |last=Böhm |first=Katharina |last2=Schmid |first2=Achim |last3=Götze |first3=Ralf |last4=Landwehr |first4=Claudia |last5=Rothgang |first5=Heinz |date=December 2013|title=Five types of OECD healthcare systems: Empirical results of a deductive classification |url=https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0168851013002285 |journal=Health Policy |language=en-US |volume=113 |issue=3 |pages=258–269 |doi=10.1016/j.healthpol.2013.09.003 |issn=0168-8510 |archive-url=http://web.archive.org/web/20240415051809/https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0168851013002285 |archive-date=2024-04-15}}</ref> Countries may achieve coverage through the [[Beveridge model]] (as in the [[United Kingdom]]), in which the government provides healthcare directly; through a single public insurer contracting with private healthcare providers (as in [[Canada]]); through mandatory social insurance provided by [[Non-governmental organization|non-governmental organizations]] (as in [[Germany]]); or through mandatory private insurance provided by for-profit companies (as in the [[Netherlands]] and [[Switzerland]]).<ref name=":5" /> Systems also differ substantially in which services are covered and what share of costs patients pay.

The [[World Health Organization]] describes universal healthcare as a situation where citizens can access health services without incurring financial hardship,<ref name="WHO 2010"/><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.who.int/mediacentre/factsheets/fs395/en/|title=Universal health coverage (UHC)|access-date=November 30, 2016}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal|last=Matheson|first=Don *|date=2015-01-01|title=Will Universal Health Coverage (UHC) Lead to the Freedom to Lead Flourishing and Healthy Lives? Comment on "Inequities in the Freedom to Lead a Flourishing and Healthy Life: Issues for Healthy Public Policy"|journal=International Journal of Health Policy and Management|volume=4|issue=1|pages=49–51|doi=10.15171/ijhpm.2015.09|pmid=25584354|pmc=4289038}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal|last1=Abiiro|first1=Gilbert Abotisem|last2=De Allegri|first2=Manuela|date=2015-07-04|title=Universal health coverage from multiple perspectives: a synthesis of conceptual literature and global debates|journal=BMC International Health and Human Rights|volume=15|article-number=17|doi=10.1186/s12914-015-0056-9|pmid=26141806|pmc=4491257|issn=1472-698X |doi-access=free }}</ref> and [[Sustainable Development Goals#Goal 3: Good health and well-being|Sustainable Development Goal 3]] includes universal health coverage.<ref name="World Health Organization 2016">{{cite web | title=Universal health coverage (UHC) | website=World Health Organization | date=12 December 2016 | url=https://www.who.int/mediacentre/factsheets/fs395/en/ | access-date=14 September 2017}}</ref>

